<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>name</key>
  <string>Darkberry Blackwater</string>
  <key>comment</key>
  <string>
Darkberry Blackwater for bat (and any other syntect host: delta, gitui, Sublime Text). Darkest. Deeper wine surfaces, same accents as Mire.

Generated by build.mjs from src/ports/bat.tmTheme. Do not edit ports/bat/.
Install with:  cp "Darkberry Blackwater.tmTheme" "$(bat --config-dir)/themes/"   (~/.config/bat/themes/)
               bat cache --build
then  bat --theme="Darkberry Blackwater" &lt;file&gt;,  or  export BAT_THEME="Darkberry Blackwater",  or add
--theme="Darkberry Blackwater" to $(bat --config-dir)/config. Keep the file name as it is: bat lists
the theme under the name element inside the file, and `bat --list-themes` shows it.
Re-run `bat cache --build` after replacing the file; bat reads the cache, not the folder.
Checked on bat 0.26.1; written against bat 0.24, whose cache directory is
$(bat --cache-dir). Needs a truecolor terminal (COLORTERM=truecolor) or bat rounds every
colour to the nearest xterm-256 index.

What bat reads from the settings block: foreground (the default text), gutterForeground
(line numbers, the grid and the rule) and lineHighlight (the fill behind
--highlight-line). background is used only by hosts that paint one (delta, Sublime); bat
leaves the terminal's own background, which is why every syntax role is checked against
ui.background. caret, selection, findHighlight, gutter, guide and accent are for the
other hosts and follow the same roles as the Kate and VS Code ports.

Deliberately left unset:
- selectionForeground: syntax keeps its own colour on the selection (ui.selection is
  checked to carry code at 65% of each role's minimum).
- inactiveSelection: no role for it yet (STYLE_GUIDE.md, meaning table).
- uuid: syntect ignores it and Sublime writes its own.
- bat's git gutter marks (+ ~ _): bat hardcodes them to ANSI green, red and yellow, so
  they follow the terminal port (kitty, Ghostty, Konsole), not this file.
- Markdown link text (meta.link.inline.description.markdown) and the language name after
  a fence (constant.other.language-name.markdown): bat's Markdown syntax scopes them under
  names Catppuccin's list never sets, so they stay ui.text. The scope list here is
  Catppuccin's, mapped through the roles, and adds nothing to it.
- Language-specific rules Catppuccin carries (C++, C#, CSS, GraphQL, Julia, LaTeX,
  Liquid, Lua, Nix, PHP, most of Python and Rust): their meanings are already covered by
  the generic scope each of them refines, through the same syntax.* role.

Host-owned pairings: none. Every fill here that carries text is either checked by the
build (ui.line.current and ui.selection carry code; ui.mark.text on ui.mark1) or is
painted under a foreground this file also sets.

Differs from Catppuccin:
- Headings are one colour, syntax.function, as in the VS Code and Kate ports, not a
  rainbow by level (red, peach, yellow, green, sapphire, lavender).
- Functions, classes, types, parameters and decorators are upright; Catppuccin
  italicises them. Only comments, markup.italic and a strikethrough carry a font style,
  which is what the VS Code template does.
- Rust primitives (u32, str) read as keywords: bat's bundled Rust syntax scopes them and
  `let` alike as storage.type.rust, so the `int is syntax.type` rule cannot reach them
  without recolouring `let`. Catppuccin makes the same choice (mauve for both).
- Booleans, null and `this`/`self` take syntax.keyword and syntax.variable (VS Code's
  mapping), not Catppuccin's peach and red.
- Diff changed lines are ui.warning, the meaning table's colour for "modified", and
  removed lines are syntax.diff.removed (a readable mix), not plain red.
- Markdown link titles and references are syntax.link, one link colour, not lavender.
- List bullets, separators and fenced-code fences are punctuation, not teal.
  </string>
